---
import ArticleDemoCallout from "../../../components/ArticleDemoCallout.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import pages from "../../../data/pages.json";
---

<SiteLayout metadata={pages["/blog/soap-vs-dap-notes/"]}>
  <main id="main" class="growth-page">
    <div class="wrap">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>{" "}
        <a href="/tools/">Resources</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>{" "}
        <span aria-current="page">SOAP or DAP? Work through the format.</span>
      </nav>
      <header class="growth-hero">
        <div class="eyebrow">Documentation · By Otto editorial</div>
        <h1>SOAP or DAP? Work through the format.</h1>
        <p>
          We compare how each format organizes the same information, so you can
          choose an approach that fits your practice’s documentation
          requirements.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="byline">
        <span>
          By <a href="/authors/otto-editorial/">Otto editorial</a> ·{" "}
          <time datetime="2026-10-16">October 16, 2026</time> · 4 min read
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="post-layout">
        <aside class="toc">
          <div class="toc-inner">
            <h2>Companion resources</h2>
            <a href="/templates/soap-notes/">SOAP note template</a>
            <a href="/templates/dap-notes/">DAP note template</a>
            <ArticleDemoCallout />
          </div>
        </aside>
        <article>
          <div class="growth-answer">
            <strong>The practical difference</strong>
            <p>
              SOAP separates Subjective and Objective information. DAP brings
              those observations and reports together under Data. Both include
              an Assessment and Plan.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="growth-section prose">
            <h2>We start with the information you need to document.</h2>
            <p>
              A format gives the note a structure. Your clinical setting,
              documentation policy, payer requirements, and the encounter
              determine what the record needs to contain. We’d choose the
              structure that helps you make those distinctions consistently.
            </p>
            <div class="table-scroll">
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Information</th>
                    <th>SOAP</th>
                    <th>DAP</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Client-reported experience</td>
                    <td>Subjective</td>
                    <td>Data</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Observations, measures, interventions, and response</td>
                    <td>
                      Objective or the appropriate section under your
                      documentation policy
                    </td>
                    <td>Data</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Clinical interpretation and progress</td>
                    <td>Assessment</td>
                    <td>Assessment</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>Agreed next steps</td>
                    <td>Plan</td>
                    <td>Plan</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <h2>Try the same fictional session in both.</h2>
            <p>
              Our examples follow a fictional client practicing a response to
              work messages. In SOAP, we separate what the client reports from
              what happens in the session. In DAP, those details share a Data
              section. The assessment still needs the clinician’s reasoning in
              either format.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="resource-cards">
            <a class="resource-card" href="/templates/soap-notes/">
              <span class="tag">Four-part format</span>
              <h3>SOAP note template</h3>
              <p>
                Separate subjective and objective information, then document
                your assessment and plan.
              </p>
              <span class="text-link">Explore SOAP ↗</span>
            </a>
            <a class="resource-card" href="/templates/dap-notes/">
              <span class="tag">Three-part format</span>
              <h3>DAP note template</h3>
              <p>Organize the session into Data, Assessment, and Plan.</p>
              <span class="text-link">Explore DAP ↗</span>
            </a>
          </div>
          <div class="growth-section prose">
            <h2>Three questions we would use to choose</h2>
            <ol>
              <li>
                Does the format meet the documentation requirements that apply
                to your work?
              </li>
              <li>
                Can another authorized clinician follow the encounter and the
                next steps?
              </li>
              <li>
                Can you distinguish the client’s account, observed information,
                and your assessment without duplicating whole paragraphs?
              </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
              There is no universal winning format. Test a fictional encounter
              in each, review it with the appropriate clinical owner, and use
              the structure your practice approves.
            </p>
            <h2>How this relates to Otto</h2>
            <p>
              Otto’s public workflow describes SOAP and DAP drafts from session
              audio, followed by clinician review and signature. A draft still
              needs checking against the encounter. Our template examples are
              editorial illustrations; they are not generated by the Otto
              product.
            </p>
            <a class="button" href="/demo/#watch">
              Watch Otto’s workflow ↗
            </a>
          </div>
          <div class="sources">
            <h2>Sources and review</h2>
            <p>
              Sources checked October 16, 2026. Confirm current product terms
              before choosing software.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://www.simplepractice.com/resource/soap-note-examples/">
                  SOAP note format
                </a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://www.mentalyc.com/blog/dap-notes">
                  DAP note format
                </a>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
    <section class="cta-section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="cta">
          <div>
            <h2>Make more room for the work you love.</h2>
            <p>
              See how Otto brings notes, scheduling, telehealth, and payments
              into your practice day.
            </p>
          </div>
          <a class="button light" href="/waitlist/">
            Join the waitlist <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
